// In CPP, the Standard Template Library (STL) is a powerful set of C++ template classes to provide general-purpose classes and functions with templates that implement many popular and commonly used algorithms and data structures like vectors, lists, queues, and stacks. STL has four components: Algorithms, Containers, Functions, and Iterators.
// There are four main components of the Standard Template Library (STL) in C++:
// 1. Algorithms: STL provides a collection of built-in algorithms that can be used to perform various operations on data structures. These algorithms include sorting, searching, merging, and more. They are designed to work with different types of containers and can be easily applied to user-defined data types as well.
// 2. Containers: Containers are data structures that store collections of objects. STL provides several types of containers, including vectors, lists, deques, sets, maps, and more. Each container has its own characteristics and is suitable for different use cases. For example, vectors are dynamic arrays that allow random access to elements, while lists are doubly linked lists that allow efficient insertion and deletion of elements.
// 3. Functions: STL provides a set of function objects (also known as functors) that can be used to customize the behavior of algorithms. Function objects are objects that can be called as if they were functions. They can be used to define custom comparison functions for sorting, filtering, and other operations.
// 4. Iterators: Iterators are objects that provide a way to access the elements of a container sequentially without exposing the underlying representation of the container. STL provides several types of iterators, including input iterators, output iterators, forward iterators, bidirectional iterators, and random access iterators. Each type of iterator has its own capabilities and is suitable for different use cases.